    "rationale": "Response to the round-1 critique. I reject the draft's bundled change and keep the raced best design as the base.\n\nEvidence check. The outcome table is the authoritative record. The best raced design is iteration 6 (score 465.03): base_speed 1.6, kp 2.5, ki 0.2, kd 0.8, 16 mm sensors at x = -0.04..+0.04 (0.02 m pitch), y = 0.04, mass 0.8, body 0.18 x 0.12 m, wheel track 0.12 m, radius 0.03 m. My draft (entity0) describes iteration 6 as base 0.9, kp 2.0, kd 1.0, ki 0, mass 0.6, body 0.16 and y 0.08. Those values are the draft's own untested proposal, not the raced design, so its baseline and its 'best observed' claims are wrong. Critique point 2 of the draft, that the sensor row move and speed change were confounded, is correct as a general principle, but the draft's real problem is that its base is misidentified.\n\nThe clean trend in the raced data is kd alone at base 1.6, kp 2.5, ki 0.2. kd 0.2 scored 52.11, kd 0.4 scored 220.09, kd 0.6 scored 356.57 and kd 0.8 scored 465.03. Oval finish rate rose 0, 30, 85, 95 percent. Steering oscillation rose with kd (oval 0.07 to 0.27), but the scores still rose, so the data favour damping over a noise-amplification reading, and the critique's concern is real but not yet shown to bite.\n\nWhat I kept, changed and rejected:\n- Kept: base_speed 1.6 (the speed at which oval finishes were achieved, mean 1.94 s, which leaves a margin under 3 s), kp 2.5, ki 0.2, line_loss last_direction, and all geometry from iteration 6.\n- Changed: kd 0.8 to 1.0, and nothing else. This extends the one trend the data support, and it is a single-variable change against the best raced design, so any score change can be attributed to kd.\n- Rejected: the draft's base_speed 0.9 (never raced with a good controller; at 0.9 the oval margin under 3 s shrinks and the failing tracks' progress could fall), kp 2.4 (the critique notes its effect is unquantified and it was bundled with other changes), ki 0 (removes the only term that can offset the motor mismatch), mass 0.6 and body 0.16 and sensor row y 0.08 (no race evidence; each would be a separate test).\n\nExpected results (hypotheses, not data): oval finish rate stays at 85 percent or higher, and s-bend and slalom progress rise above their iteration 6 values (0.71 and 0.16). Score rises modestly, possibly toward 480 to 520 if the trend holds. Main risk: kd 1.0 raises steering oscillation and control effort enough to push the failing tracks off the line sooner, or to cut oval completion. Decision rule for the next round: if the score exceeds 465.03 and oval completion stays at 85 percent or more, kd 1.0 becomes the base. If the score falls, kd 0.8 is the ceiling for this gain set, and the next single-variable test should be the motor-mismatch offset via a small ki.",
